1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,630
Glass on the web has always looked a little fake.

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:06,070
A blur, a border, done.

3
00:00:06,730 --> 00:00:08,400
liquidGL makes it real.

4
00:00:08,830 --> 00:00:11,620
Glass that actually bends what's behind it.

5
00:00:12,730 --> 00:00:17,020
The catch? Browsers won't let the GPU read live screen pixels.

6
00:00:17,070 --> 00:00:18,440
It's a security rule.

7
00:00:20,230 --> 00:00:24,240
So liquidGL takes its own high resolution snapshot of the page,

8
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:26,620
hands it to a shader, and bends it.

9
00:00:26,950 --> 00:00:29,300
Strongest at the edges, like a real bevel.

10
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:32,680
You control the look.

11
00:00:33,030 --> 00:00:35,560
Refraction. Bevel. Frost.

12
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:38,120
Moving highlights. Soft shadows.

13
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:41,070
Version three adds tinted glass,

14
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:43,050
true colour dispersion at the edges,

15
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:45,650
and a fluid surface that follows your finger.

16
00:00:48,730 --> 00:00:51,640
Run as many lenses as you like. Stack them.

17
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:54,240
They all draw through one shared canvas.

18
00:00:55,730 --> 00:00:56,940
It renders with WebGPU,

19
00:00:56,990 --> 00:01:01,160
and falls back to WebGL, then CSS, automatically.

20
00:01:02,230 --> 00:01:05,280
Install it. Point it at an element. That's it.

21
00:01:08,027 --> 00:01:11,463
liquidGL. Open source, MIT licensed.

22
00:01:11,825 --> 00:01:15,000
Try the demos at liquidgl.naughtyduk.com
